  "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=15$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈18.86$ and $n=15.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈15\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 18.86 \\\\  & =15\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -18.86(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈15+18.86=33.86.\n$$",
